     1	# Silent Collapse: A Distribution-Shift Teardown of a Production Driving Model and a Zero-Retraining Recurrent-State Monitor
     2	
     3	**Yusuf Guenena**
     4	Wayne State University
     5	Code and data: https://github.com/yusufdxb/supercombo-blindspot
     6	
     7	---
     8	
     9	## Abstract
    10	
    11	Production Level-2 driver-assistance stacks are validated largely in simulation, and that
    12	practice rests on an unstated assumption: that the shipped model behaves the same on rendered
    13	input as on real input, or at least signals when it does not. We test that assumption on one
    14	deployed model, openpilot v0.9.7 supercombo, the network that drives comma hardware on public
    15	roads. We first build a parity-exact reimplementation of its inference path, verified to within
    16	+/-0.5 m/s^2 of comma's own reference output on 100% of 1159 real-footage frames (median
    17	absolute delta 0.0409 m/s^2), so that any downstream anomaly is attributable to the model and
    18	not to our harness. Running the verified model on CARLA-rendered clean roads, we find that it
    19	fails silently: 8 of 10 output heads collapse to under 1% of their real-driving temporal
    20	activity and the 512-D recurrent feature freezes to about 1e-5 of its real spread, while the
    21	model's own predictive-uncertainty heads rise only 1.20x to 1.84x and not one out-of-distribution
    22	frame (0 of 219) crosses the real-driving 95th percentile. Nothing the model emits flags the
    23	collapse. An alpha-blend sweep characterizes the collapse as a hard cliff on the Subaru source
    24	(transition width 0.015), though the cliff shape is segment-dependent (a gradient of width 0.274
    25	on the RAM source), and a layer-by-layer probe localizes it downstream of the vision encoder, in
    26	the recurrent summarizer and action-block feedback path, not in perception. We then show that the
    27	signal the outputs hide is recoverable from the model's own recurrent feature with a single
    28	second-order statistic, the rolling temporal spread of the 512-D state: one O(d) quantity per
    29	forward pass, with no retraining and no architecture change. Calibrated leave-one-corpus-out to
    30	about a 1% real-driving false-positive rate (N=2, a two-fold estimate, not a production FPR), it
    31	separates the collapse at AUROC 0.996 [0.992, 1.000] and fires about 0.23 blend-units before the
    32	outputs cliff, where the location-based feature scores one would default to (Mahalanobis,
    33	Relative Mahalanobis, KNN) each hit 100% leave-one-corpus-out FPR and fail to transfer across the
    34	two real corpora. This is a single-model negative finding with a collapse-specific monitor, not a
    35	general OOD detector: an ImageNet-C sweep shows the silent collapse is sim-specific (no corruption
    36	reproduces it; at most 1 of 10 heads collapses on any of 75 corruption-severity cells, versus 7
    37	of 10 under CARLA), and the monitor is near chance on most photometric corruptions. The
    38	contribution is that output-side and location-based signals alone are insufficient for this one
    39	shipped model's safety case, and that a second-order recurrent-state monitor is a cheap complement
    40	that the present evidence does not claim generalizes.
   270	state handling.
   271	
   272	**Unnormalized YUV input.** The model's input loader (loadyuv) converts the camera Y, U, and V
   273	channels to float with no rescaling: the model consumes uint8 values in the range 0 to 255, not
   274	values divided by 255. Dividing by 255, the reflexive normalization, shifts the entire input
   275	distribution and silently degrades parity. We feed unnormalized uint8 YUV, matching the kernel.
   276	
   277	**Parity result.** On 1159 real-footage frames (after a 40-frame, 2-second warm-up trim), our
   278	reimplemented longitudinal-acceleration output matches comma's logged reference within +/-0.5 m/s^2
   279	on 100.00% of frames, with a median absolute delta of 0.0409 m/s^2, a mean of 0.0541, and a worst-case
   280	single-frame delta of 0.2899 m/s^2 (no frame exceeds 0.5). This is the load-bearing harness-trust
   281	claim for the negative result, and we report it prominently.
   282	
   283	**Data.** The real in-distribution data are two comma corpora, denoted subaru and ram, of 320 frames
   284	each, with the first 100 frames discarded as warm-up. After warm-up and the rolling-window step, 219
   285	analysis frames remain per corpus. The out-of-distribution data are CARLA-rendered clean-road frames
   286	from the openpilot v0.9.7 simulation pipeline. The sim camera is configured as a matched-intrinsics
   287	pinhole: it renders at the comma 3 fcam native resolution (1928x1208) at the field of view that
   288	reproduces fcam's focal length (2648 px), so the rendered frames carry exactly the production camera
   289	(`_ar_ox_config.fcam`) intrinsics, and because the sim camera is mounted on the device axes (zero
   290	extrinsic calibration), its warp to the model frame reduces to the same intrinsic remap
   291	(`K_fcam @ inv(K_medmodel)`) that the real path applies after its `liveCalibration` extrinsics
   292	(`src/sim_preprocessor.py`). The intrinsics and the model-input preprocessing are therefore identical
   293	to the parity-verified real path, and the distribution shift is confined to rendered image content
   294	(photometry, texture, and the absence of sensor noise); the collapse is a response to rendered scene
   295	content, not an artifact of a mismatched camera model. The interpolation axis for the cliff analysis
   296	(Section 5.4) is a pixel-space alpha-blend of a real sequence (Subaru or RAM) with the CARLA sequence,
   297	with alpha=0 the real frame and alpha=1 the CARLA frame, swept over 29 alpha values. We state the
   298	underlying counts wherever a percentage appears: for example, the "0%" of Section 5.3 is 0 of 219
   299	CARLA frames. For the threshold-free metrics (Section 5.6), the in-distribution set is subaru and ram
   300	concatenated (n=638 stored frames) and the out-of-distribution set is the alpha=1.0 CARLA frames
   301	(n=319 stored frames); the rolling-window warm-up leaves 609 and 290 valid (non-NaN) scores
   302	respectively, and the threshold-free metrics are computed on those valid subsets.
   303	
   304	**Metrics.** Output activity is the sum of per-element temporal standard deviation over a window; a
   305	head is "collapsed" when its CARLA-to-real activity ratio is small. Feature spread is the trace of the
   365	### 5.3 E3: Uncertainty silence
   366	
   367	This is the safety-relevant centerpiece. For three representative heads we report, side by side, the
   368	fraction of output activity retained on CARLA, the ratio of CARLA to real predictive uncertainty, and
   369	the fraction of the 219 CARLA frames that exceed the real-driving 95th-percentile uncertainty for that
   370	head. The plan head retains 0.6% of its activity while its uncertainty rises 1.35x; the lead head
   371	retains 0.4% at 1.20x; the desired_curv head retains 0.2% at 1.84x. In all three cases, 0 of 219 CARLA
   372	frames exceeds the real-driving p95. The outputs lose roughly 99.5% of their activity, but the
   373	uncertainty channel barely moves and never crosses the threshold a real-calibrated monitor would set.
   374	
   375	The implication is the gap this paper turns on. A safety monitor that thresholds the model's own
   376	uncertainty, calibrated on real driving, never fires under the collapse, because nothing the model
   377	emits flags it. We state this strictly as an empirical finding about supercombo v0.9.7 under CARLA
   378	input, that the uncertainty channel stays quiet, and we draw no causal line from it to any field
   379	phantom-braking incident. Figure E3 plots the uncertainty distributions for real and CARLA frames
   380	against the real-driving p95 line, making the silence visually unambiguous.
   381	
   382	### 5.4 E4: Cliff characterization and segment dependence
   383	
   384	To characterize the shape of the collapse, we sweep the pixel-space alpha-blend from the real frame
   385	(alpha=0) toward the CARLA frame (alpha=1). On the Subaru source the response is two-phase. Output
   386	activity first balloons to 6.32x of the real baseline at alpha=0.425, a thrash driven by the
   387	ghosted-input interference of the half-blended frame, and then collapses through a hard cliff, falling
   388	from 0.9x to 0.1x of real activity over the narrow alpha band 0.784 to 0.799, a transition width of
   389	0.015. The feature spread crashes from 0.25 to 0.00 by about alpha=0.78. Through the entire transition
   390	the predictive uncertainty never spikes, consistent with E3.
   391	
   392	The cliff shape is segment-dependent, and this is a bound on the early-warning headroom the monitor
   393	exploits. On the RAM source the same sweep produces a gradient, not a cliff: output activity falls from
   394	0.9x to 0.1x of real over the wide alpha band 0.666 to 0.940, a transition width of 0.274. On the RAM
   395	source the monitor's firing point (alpha=0.850) lands inside that band rather than before it, so the
   396	early-warning headroom is negative (-0.184) where on Subaru it is positive (0.234). Cliff headroom
   397	therefore cannot be assumed to generalize across segments; the characterization is partial. We use the
   398	authoritative `report/e4_results.md` and `report/e4_ram_results.md` values (Subaru width 0.015, RAM
   399	width 0.274) throughout; where a figure legend rounds these (for example a Subaru legend reading
   400	"0.02"), the results-file value governs. Figure E4 places the Subaru cliff and the RAM gradient side by
   401	side.
   402	
   403	### 5.5 E5: Localization downstream of the vision encoder
   404	
   405	We first ask whether the collapse is the vision encoder failing, by measuring the CARLA-to-real
   415	the monitor reads) has a cliff at alpha=0.900, with its mean shifting to 0.023 of real, a near
   416	two-order-of-magnitude collapse of the rolling mean of the 512-D vector; this is the entry point of the
   417	collapse. The action-block body (action_block_body), the last residual block before the curvature head,
   418	has a cliff a full alpha step earlier, at alpha=0.500, because it folds in the model's own previous
   419	desired-curvature output through the recurrent feedback loop, so once that already-collapsing signal
   420	joins, the action stack saturates fast. The transformer self-attention, feed-forward, and reduce-sum
   421	stages track the summarizer to within 2 to 11% and introduce no additional collapse; they are passive
   422	relays. The post-encoder projection (vision_post, 1.89x at alpha=1) and the non-temporal hydra trunk
   423	(hydra_trunk, 2.71x at alpha=1) show no cliff, consistent with the two surviving heads of E1. The
   424	localization is partial: the summarizer ends in a mu-over-sigma variational reparameterization, and we
   425	have not separated the mu path from the sigma normalization, so part of the apparent summarizer collapse
   426	could be variance normalization rather than information loss. We state this ambiguity rather than claim
   427	a complete mechanistic account. Figures E5a and E5b show the per-stage and per-submodule ratios.
   428	
   429	### 5.6 E6: Monitor detection and baseline comparison
   430	
   431	We now test whether the signal the outputs hide is recoverable from the recurrent feature, and how a
   432	second-order monitor of that feature compares to the location-based scores one would default to.
   433	
   434	The threshold-free comparison is at alpha=1.0 (full CARLA shift), on the in-distribution set of subaru
   435	and ram concatenated (n=638 stored frames) against the CARLA OOD frames (n=319 stored frames), with
   436	stratified bootstrap 95% confidence intervals; after the rolling-window warm-up, the metrics are
   437	computed on the 609 and 290 valid scores that remain. The rolling-spread monitor reaches AUROC
   438	0.996 [0.992, 1.000], AUPR 0.995 [0.990, 1.000], and FPR at 95% TPR of 0.000. KNN-50 reaches AUROC
   439	1.000 [1.000, 1.000]. We do not claim to beat KNN-50: on single-corpus separation the two tie. Relative
   440	Mahalanobis reaches AUROC 0.934 [0.914, 0.952]. Mahalanobis (0.159 [0.130, 0.190]) and PCA-Mahalanobis
   441	(0.152 [0.124, 0.179]) both score below chance. Table E6 collects these with their confidence intervals.
   442	
   443	Mahalanobis scores below chance for a mechanical reason worth stating, not just reporting. Under the
   444	collapse the recurrent state freezes to a near-constant vector that lands near the center of the
   445	in-distribution Gaussian (E2), so the OOD frames receive lower Mahalanobis distance than the real
   446	frames: a distance-from-mean score cannot detect collapse-to-the-mean, and inverts. PCA-Mahalanobis
   447	inherits the same inversion.
   448	
   449	The distinguishing axis is not single-corpus separation but cross-corpus calibration, and here the
   450	location-based scores fail to transfer. Under leave-one-corpus-out across subaru and ram, all three
   451	applicable location-based baselines (Mahalanobis, relative Mahalanobis, KNN-50) hit 100% LOCO FPR: a
   452	threshold calibrated on one corpus flags the entirety of the other. The PCA-Mahalanobis variant improves
   453	to 11.91% LOCO mean FPR, a partial recovery but still an order of magnitude above the 1% operating point.
   454	The rolling-spread monitor holds 1.03% LOCO mean FPR (max 2.07%), where we emphasize that this is an N=2
   455	two-fold estimate, not a production false-positive rate. The geometric reason the location-based scores
   456	fail is that the subaru and ram corpora occupy disjoint regions of the 512-D feature space whose
   457	inter-corpus separation dwarfs the within-corpus radius (visible as the two real clusters in Figure E2),
   458	so any absolute-position score calibrated on one corpus flags the whole of the other. The monitor reads
   459	the second-order trace, which is location-invariant, so it both separates and calibrates across the
   460	corpus shift.
   461	
   462	The monitor also fires before the outputs cliff. Sweeping alpha, the monitor's fired fraction crosses
   463	50% at alpha=0.550, where the E4 Subaru output cliff is at about alpha=0.784, an early-warning gap of
   464	about 0.23 blend-units. (As Section 5.4 notes, this headroom is Subaru-specific; on the RAM gradient the
   465	firing point falls inside the transition band.) Figures E6a and E6b show the fire rate and the AUROC
   466	trajectory across alpha, and Figure E6c gives the ROC and PR curves at alpha=1.0.
   467	
   468	The honest positioning against the nearest neighbors follows from these numbers. Against the
   469	location-based family (Keser-style density, Lee Mahalanobis, Ren RMD, Sun KNN), the result is not that
   470	the monitor separates better, since KNN-50 ties it, but that the location-based scores fail to transfer
   471	across corpora (100% LOCO FPR) while the second-order monitor calibrates (about 1%, N=2). Against
   472	EigenTrack (arXiv:2509.15735), the nearest mechanism-twin, the deltas are substrate (a shipped driving
   473	model, not an LLM or VLM), statistic (a single location-invariant spread trace with a calibrated
   474	threshold, not a full eigenspectrum fed to a trained classifier), and evaluation axis (cross-corpus LOCO
   475	FPR on real-driving frames, not language OOD benchmarks). We frame the result as transfer and
   485	with a validation gate that first reproduces the published CARLA collapse (7 of 10 heads) before any
   486	corruption result is read.
   487	
   488	**The collapse is sim-specific.** Running the same E1 output-collapse metric on every corruption-severity
   489	cell, no ImageNet-C corruption reproduces the output collapse: 0 of 75 cells reach the collapse criterion
   490	(5 or more of 10 heads), and the maximum on any single cell is 1 of 10 heads, against 7 of 10 under CARLA.
   491	The silent collapse is a property of full-sim rendering, not of photometric or blur corruptions of real
   492	frames. One consequence is immediate: there are zero false negatives, because there is no output collapse
   493	anywhere in the sweep for the monitor to miss, so its quiet response on fog, brightness, blur, and the
   494	rest is correct.
   495	
